\subsection{Pressure Poisson Equation}

The pressure equation given in Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss} is re-arranged to match the form of Eq. \ref{eq:Poiss}, as shown in Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss_rearr}, following the convention of Ref. \cite{Zhang_MA615_2020}. Expanding Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss_rearr} and evaluating at a given pair of ($m$, $n$) indices yields Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss_sub}. The right-hand side of Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss_sub} is set to the term $R_{m,n}$ for convenience; Eq. \ref{eq:R}. The predictor-step velocity terms in Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss_sub} are solved with 2nd-order accurate central differencing schemes as described in Eq. \ref{eq:deriv_u1pred_x1}, \ref{eq:deriv_u2pred_x2}. The second derivatives of pressure on the left-hand side of the equation in Eq. \ref{eq:diff_press_poiss_sub} are also approximated to second-order spatial accuracy with central differencing schemes shown in Eqs. \ref{eq:lap_p_x1}, \ref{eq:lap_p_x2}. Following the convention of \cite{Zhang_MA615_2020}, we define the $NxN$ matrix, ${L_{x_{1}}}$ as the matrix representation of the Laplacian operator in the $x_{1}$ direction expressed in Eq. \ref{eq:lap_p_x1} as shown in Eq. \ref{eq:Lx1}. Because the problem domain is square ($N_{x_{1}} = N_{x_{2}} = N, \Delta{x_{1}} = \Delta{x_{2}} = \Delta{x}$), the matrix representation of the Laplacian operator in the $x_{2}$ direction, $L_{x_{2}}$, is identical to $L_{x_{1}}$, as shown in Eq. \ref{eq:Lx2}. Matrices $I_{x_{1}}$ and $I_{x_{2}}$, which are identity matrices of size $(N_{x_{1}} = N) x (N_{x_{1}} = N)$ and $(N_{x_{2}} = N) x (N_{x_{2}} = N)$, are defined and shown in Eqs. \ref{eq:Ix1}, \ref{eq:Ix2}. This Poisson equation problem re-cast in matrix representation is shown in Eq. \ref{eq:Poisson_mat}. In Eq. \ref{eq:Possion_mat}, the ``$\bigotimes$'' symbol denoted the Kronecker product, and ``$\text{vec}$'' denotes vectorized forms of a matrix in which the vector is formed by stacking the columns of the matrix. 

\begin{equation}
  -\frac{\partial^{2}\phi}{\partial{x}^{2}} = f
  \label{eq:Poiss}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
    -\frac{\partial}{\partial{x_{i}}}\bigg(\frac{\partial{p}}{\partial{x_{i}}}\bigg) = 
    - \text{St}\frac{1}{\Delta{t}}\frac{\partial{u_{i}^{*}}}{\partial{x_{i}}}
  }
  \label{eq:diff_press_poiss_rearr}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  -\bigg[\frac{\partial^{2}{p_{(m,n)}}}{\partial{x_{1,m}^{2}}} + \frac{\partial^{2}{p_{(m,n)}}}{\partial{x_{2,n}^{2}}}\bigg] = -\text{St}\frac{1}{\Delta{t}}\bigg[\frac{\partial{u_{1,(m,n)}^{*}}}{\partial{x_{1,m}^{*}}} + \frac{\partial{u_{2,(m,n)}^{*}}}{\partial{x_{2,n}}^{*}}\bigg] 
  \label{eq:diff_press_poiss_sub}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  R_{m,n} = -\text{St}\frac{1}{\Delta{t}}\bigg[\frac{\partial{u_{1,(m,n)}^{*}}}{\partial{x_{1,m}^{*}}} + \frac{\partial{u_{2,(m,n)}^{*}}}{\partial{x_{2,n}}^{*}}\bigg] 
